# Telegram Channel Content Audit — WAY2WEI Case Study

*[Русская версия ниже / Russian version below](#русская-версия)*

A full-cycle content analytics project: scraping a company's public Telegram channel, classifying 176 posts across six years, and turning raw text into a strategic content audit — used as self-directed research ahead of a marketing interview, not a generic exercise.

## Why this project

Before interviewing for a B2B marketing role at a HR-tech company, I wanted to go beyond reading the company website — I wanted to understand their actual communication pattern over time: what they talk about, how often, and where the gaps are. Rather than guessing, I parsed and analyzed their own public channel.

## Data & Method

- **Source:** public Telegram channel, 176 posts, April 2020 – September 2026 (exported via a Colab scraping tool: `id`, `date`, `text`, `url`)
- **Tooling:** Python (pandas, `re`), no ML — the analysis deliberately relies on transparent, auditable heuristics rather than a black-box classifier, so every finding can be traced back to a specific regex rule or count
- **Techniques applied:**
  - Time-series analysis of posting cadence (by year, month, weekday, hour)
  - Rule-based content categorization (7 categories: product/CTA, educational, client case study, event, research, etc.) with cross-tabulation by year to detect strategy drift
  - Keyword-frequency comparison between early-period (2020–2023) and recent-period (2025–2026) vocabulary to surface a tonal shift
  - Industry/topic coverage mapping against the company's own stated target segments (from their website) to find uncovered niches
  - External-link and hashtag-usage audit

## Key findings

- Posting volume was flat for three years (13–19 posts/year, 2020–2023), then grew ~2.5x starting 2024 — a clear inflection point in content investment.
- Content mix shifted materially: in 2026, ~53% of posts carry a direct CTA (up from a roughly even 3-way split between CTA / educational / case-study content in 2025) — while client case studies, historically the strongest trust-building format, nearly disappeared (16 → 2 posts).
- Vocabulary analysis shows a shift from conceptual language ("emotion," "ability," "development") in 2020–2023 to instrumental, product-centric language ("solutions," "data," "potential," "reserve") in 2025–2026.
- Despite the company's own website naming manufacturing, hospitality, consulting and startups as target industries, the channel's content is overwhelmingly skewed toward IT (30 mentions) — a measurable gap between stated ICP and actual content focus.
- One specific, high-relevance topic — new-employee onboarding / first 90 days — was never covered directly across 176 posts, despite being directly addressed by one of the company's own products.

## Deliverables

- `WAY2WEI_TG_content_analysis.md` — full written report (8 sections: cadence, content mix, vocabulary shift, format audit, industry coverage, content gaps, recommendations)
- A sample content piece written in the channel's own voice (tone, structure, link placement) to close one of the identified gaps, as a practical demonstration of the findings rather than a purely academic exercise

## Skills demonstrated

Data wrangling and classification with pandas · time-series and categorical analysis · competitive/content-strategy auditing · translating quantitative findings into actionable marketing recommendations · bilingual (RU/EN) technical writing

## Notes

This is applied, scrappy analysis built for a real decision (interview prep), not a polished production pipeline — categorization rules are intentionally simple (regex-based) and documented inline rather than hidden behind a model, so a reader can verify or extend them in minutes.

